CAT = "cisSystems"; SEG = "SYS"
Q = [
("Under FDA's UDI system, what does the device identifier (DI) part of a UDI identify?",
 'The labeler and model', ['The patient who received it', 'The lot it was made in', 'The hospital that owns it'],
 'The DI is the fixed part of the UDI that identifies the labeler and the specific version or model. The production identifier holds lot, serial and dates.'),
('Which part of a UDI carries the lot number, serial number and expiration date?',
 'The production identifier', ['The device identifier', 'The GUDID database record', 'The catalog number'],
 'FDA describes the production identifier (PI) as the variable part of the UDI that holds lot or batch, serial number, expiration and manufacture dates.'),
("What does FDA's Global Unique Device Identification Database (GUDID) contain?",
 'Device identifiers and facts', ['Lot and serial numbers', 'Patient names and cases', 'Hospital tracking scans'],
 'GUDID holds the device identifier and a standard set of identifying elements. FDA notes that it does not include the production identifier.'),
('A count sheet is changed after a surgeon asks for a new instrument in a set. What must happen in the tracking system?',
 'Its record is updated and approved', ['It is added at the next audit', 'The old sheet stays until reprint', 'Each tech notes it on the sheet'],
 'Count sheet maintenance keeps the tracking system matching what is really in the set. Changes follow an approval process so every copy agrees.'),
('Why are tracking system software updates scheduled and tested?',
 'So scanning keeps working', ['So old records are cleared', 'So the vendor can bill it', 'So training can be skipped'],
 'Updates can change screens, data or links to sterilizers and scanners. Planning and testing them avoids lost records and delays.'),
('A technician scans a set but the system shows a different set name. What should happen?',
 'Stop and fix the mismatch', ['Send the set out anyway', 'Rename it in the system by hand', 'Scan the next set instead'],
 'A scan mismatch means a label, barcode or record is wrong. Fixing it before release keeps the case and the patient history accurate.'),
('When a loaner set arrives in SPD, what should be recorded at receipt?',
 'Arrival time, vendor and contents', ['The surgeon and case date only', "The vendor's sales invoice", 'The tray count on the box lid'],
 'Logging when the set arrived, from which vendor and what it contained supports tracking, shows whether it came in time and helps resolve missing items later.'),
('A loaner tray arrives without its cleaning and sterilization instructions. What is the right step?',
 'Get the IFU first', ["Use a similar tray's settings", 'Run it on the longest cycle', 'Send it to the OR as received'],
 'The outline names availability of IFUs as a loaner challenge. Without the IFU the validated steps and cycle are unknown, so the vendor is asked for them.'),
("When a loaner set with implants is received, what is checked against the vendor's paperwork?",
 'Every instrument and implant', ['The trays and their weights', 'The implant caddies only', "The vendor's case date"],
 "Receiving includes counting instruments and implants against the vendor's list, so missing items are found before use and implants used can be billed and replaced."),
('After surgery, what must happen to a loaner set before the vendor picks it up?',
 'It is fully decontaminated', ['It is sterilized and wrapped', 'It is returned unopened', 'It is wiped with a dry towel'],
 "The outline's post-operative loaner steps include point-of-use treatment, decontamination and restock before pickup, so the vendor is not exposed."),
('Implants were used from a loaner set during a case. What step follows?',
 'Record them and restock', ['Leave the gaps in the caddy', 'Fill gaps with any screws', 'Discard the whole caddy'],
 'Used implants are documented to the patient and reported so the vendor replaces them. The outline lists restock in the post-operative loaner process.'),
('What does deionization remove from water used in instrument processing?',
 'Charged mineral ions', ['Heat-stable endotoxin only', 'Dissolved oxygen only', 'Large debris and sand'],
 'Deionization exchanges positive and negative ions such as calcium, sodium and chloride for hydrogen and hydroxide. It does not reliably remove endotoxin, so it is often paired with other treatment.'),
("FDA's reprocessing guidance says final rinse water for some devices should be good enough to remove which contaminant?",
 'Bacterial endotoxins', ['Bone cement', 'Dissolved oxygen', 'Chlorine taste and smell'],
 'FDA says final rinse water specifications for certain devices should be sufficient to remove bacterial endotoxins, and notes that tap water may contain endotoxins.'),
('Which water quality reading rises as more dissolved minerals and salts are present?',
 'Conductivity', ['Endotoxin level', 'Temperature', 'Turbidity'],
 'Conductivity measures how well water carries current, which increases with dissolved ions. The outline lists it with pH, alkalinity and endotoxins as water parameters.'),
('What does reverse osmosis do to water used in instrument processing?',
 'Removes most dissolved solids', ['Kills bacteria with UV light', 'Softens it with salt exchange', 'Removes chlorine with carbon'],
 'Reverse osmosis forces water through a membrane that holds back most dissolved salts, organics and microbes, producing water suitable as critical water.'),
('Point-of-use water for instrument processing is commonly tested monthly for which contaminant?',
 'Endotoxin', ['Lead', 'Fluoride', 'Chlorine taste'],
 "HSPA's CIS outline gives daily visual checks and monthly endotoxin testing at the point of water use as examples of testing frequency."),
('Instruments show a white, chalky film after the final rinse. What water problem is most likely?',
 'Hard water minerals', ['Too much endotoxin', 'Low water pressure', 'Water that is too cold'],
 'Calcium and magnesium in hard water dry into white spots and film. Treated final rinse water prevents it.'),
('Water with high chloride content is used on stainless steel instruments. What damage can follow?',
 'Pitting corrosion', ['Heat tint', 'Magnetization', 'A dull satin finish'],
 'Chlorides attack the protective oxide layer of stainless steel and cause small pits. The outline lists pitting, staining and rusting as water quality impacts.'),
('Why is pH monitored in water used for instrument processing?',
 'It affects chemistry and metal', ['It shows endotoxin levels', 'It sets the rinse temperature', 'It shows hardness directly'],
 'Water that is too acidic or alkaline changes how detergents work and can attack metals. The outline lists pH among water parameters.'),
('Why should the cause of rust on many instruments be investigated rather than only replacing them?',
 'Water or steam may be the cause', ['Rust always comes from the maker', 'Rust only forms in storage', 'Rust means a failed BI'],
 'Rust across many items often comes from the process, such as poor water or steam quality or a rusting item in the load. Fixing the source stops the spread.'),
]
